## Step 6 — Logout and Session Endpoint (S0005, S0006 server)

**Logout (ADR-0082 D7)**

- Takes the same row lock as renewal.
- Revocation uses RFC 7009 with a 5 s timeout and no retry.
- The outcome is recorded in `session_event` `logout`, and the response follows `logout-result-v1`.
- An expired session gives `not_attempted` (`no_live_tokens`).
- A double submit: the second request finds the session ended and returns `not_attempted` (`session_already_ended`), so only one revocation is attempted.

**Session endpoint and active knowledge base (ADR-0082 D10)**

- `GET /session` and `PUT /session/active-knowledge-base`.
- `grant_revision` is SHA-256 over the sorted current `(membership_id, grant_revision)` pairs; revoked and expired memberships are excluded.
- Flags are read from `config/experience-flags.yaml`, with an mtime cache.

## Step 7 — Shell (S0003 AC6, S0004, S0005 AC5/AC6, S0006, S0007)

**Stack and layout**

- Stack as ADR-0082 D12.
# F0021-S0005 — Log out with upstream revocation and cache hygiene

**Story ID:** F0021-S0005
**Feature:** F0021 — Native React semantic shell + OIDC/session contract and safe re-auth behavior
**Title:** Log out with upstream revocation and cache hygiene
**Priority:** Critical
**Phase:** MVP — v0.1B
**Status:** Not Started

## User Story

**As a** security administrator (Noor)
**I want** sign-out to revoke the session at the identity provider, end it in Nebula, clear what the browser loaded, and tell the user if revocation failed
**So that** "signed out" is true, and nothing from one user's session is shown to the next.

## Context & Background

ADR-0051 D4: logout revokes the server-held refresh token at the IdP and ends the server session; a failure is reported, not hidden by clearing the cookie. D6: query caches and in-flight work are cleared on logout, principal change, tenant switch and relevant grant revision. §119.3 found the CRM revoking a token its browser flow never set.

## Acceptance Criteria

- [ ] **AC1:** "Sign out" sends a CSRF-protected request authenticated by the session cookie; the BFF revokes the session's refresh token at authentik's revocation endpoint (RFC 7009), ends the server session, and clears the session cookie (expired `Set-Cookie` with the same attributes).
- [ ] **AC2:** The token revoked is the one the session actually holds after any rotation (S0003 AC3); after logout, a refresh-token grant with that token fails at authentik.
- [ ] **AC3:** If revocation fails (IdP unreachable, non-2xx, timeout), the server session is still ended and its tokens discarded, the failure is recorded durably in the audit log with the principal and reason, and the signed-out page shows the revocation-failed warning. The response never reports success for a failed revocation.
- [ ] **AC4:** After logout, the old session identifier is rejected with HTTP 401 `unauthenticated` for every route, including when replayed from another client.
- [ ] **AC5:** On logout, principal change (S0001 AC7, S0004 AC5), active knowledge-base switch (S0006 AC4) or a change in the principal's grant revision reported by the session endpoint, the shell aborts in-flight requests and clears all query caches and in-memory resource data before rendering anything for the new state.
- [ ] **AC6:** Other open tabs of the same browser show the signed-out state on their next server call and within 60 seconds of the logout (PM assumption A8), without showing data loaded before the logout.
- [ ] **AC7:** After revocation, the BFF ends the user's IdP session through authentik's end-session endpoint, so the next "Sign in" asks for credentials; if that step fails it is reported the same way as AC3.

## Edge Cases & Error Scenarios

Logout while a renewal is in flight: logout wins and any newly issued tokens are revoked (S0003). Logout with an already-expired session: cookie cleared, signed-out page shown, no revocation attempted for discarded tokens, and the outcome says so. Double-click on "Sign out": one revocation attempt.

## Interaction Contract

Entry: "Sign out" in the shell header or the session-expired dialog's "Discard and sign out". Result: signed-out page with the revocation outcome. Persistence evidence: session record ended; revocation outcome recorded; authentik rejects the revoked refresh token. Audit: a durable record of every logout outcome, including failures (event shape is Phase B).

## Data Requirements

Logout outcome record: principal, session reference (not the cookie value), revocation result, end-session result, timestamp. Grant revision exposed by the session endpoint (S0006).

## Role-Based Visibility

Every signed-in principal can sign out. No data is shown after logout.

## Non-Functional Expectations

Revocation timeout is bounded (Phase B value) so sign-out never hangs. At least 80% changed executable-code coverage in the later feature run.

## Dependencies

S0001, S0003, S0006; authentik revocation and end-session endpoints.

## Out of Scope

Administrator-initiated session termination screens; back-channel logout from the IdP (later hardening, F0026).

## Questions & Assumptions

No open business-rule question. PM assumption A8 is in the [PRD](PRD.md#risks--assumptions). The cross-tab signal mechanism and grant-revision polling interval are Phase B decisions.

## Definition of Done

- [ ] Every AC above has a concrete observed proof in the later feature run.
- [ ] Forced upstream-failure test for AC3 (shared with S0009).
- [ ] Cache-clearing tests for each AC5 trigger.
- [ ] Documentation and tracker state reflect observed results.
- [ ] Required story-level review provenance is appended to STATUS.md in the feature action.
# F0021-S0009 — Prove session transport and mutation continuity

**Story ID:** F0021-S0009
**Feature:** F0021 — Native React semantic shell + OIDC/session contract and safe re-auth behavior
**Title:** Prove session transport and mutation continuity
**Priority:** Critical
**Phase:** MVP — v0.1B
**Status:** Not Started

## User Story

**As a** security administrator (Noor)
**I want** the session contract proven in a real browser against the real identity provider, including the failure cases
**So that** ADR-0051 is accepted on observed evidence rather than on assertions that a cookie was cleared.

## Context & Background

ADR-0051's formal acceptance needs master blueprint §121.2 items 8 (session transport) and 9 (mutation continuity) to pass against the real issuance, storage, renewal and logout flow, including an upstream revocation failure. §119.3: test logout with actual issuance, storage and renewal, not only a cookie-clear response. ADR-0049 leaves items 8 and 9 open for F0021.

## Acceptance Criteria

- [ ] **AC1:** An end-to-end suite runs a real browser against the local stack (authentik from the dev blueprint, PostgreSQL, engine, BFF, built shell) and, after sign-in, renewal and logout, inspects cookies, `localStorage`, `sessionStorage`, IndexedDB, Cache Storage, URLs and response bodies: no access, refresh or ID token appears, and the session cookie has the S0001 AC2 attributes.
- [ ] **AC2:** The suite forces access-token expiry and fires at least 20 parallel requests; authentik observes exactly one refresh grant for the session, and the rotated refresh token is the one later revoked at logout.
- [ ] **AC3:** The suite forces an upstream revocation failure (IdP revocation endpoint unreachable or erroring) and observes the server session ended, the failure recorded, and the revocation-failed warning shown; with revocation available, it observes the revoked refresh token rejected by authentik afterwards.
- [ ] **AC4:** In one browser, principal A signs out and principal B signs in (and separately, principal B completes a popup re-authentication on A's page): no data, cache entry or blocked request from A is shown to or sent for B; and every tested malicious return route lands on `/`.
- [ ] **AC5:** Mutation continuity with the rehosted Review Panel: the session is expired while a reviewer has an unsent decision; the decision is not received by the engine until "Submit now"; after re-confirmation the engine checks current item state and authority, and a variant where the item became stale or the grant was revoked during the gap is refused without retry.

## Edge Cases & Error Scenarios

The suite runs from a clean volume and is repeatable; a flaky IdP start is a harness failure, not a pass. Results record authentik and browser versions.

## Interaction Contract

Entry: the proof harness command run by a developer or CI job. Result: a pass/fail report with per-check evidence (storage dumps, IdP event logs, engine audit rows) stored in the feature run's evidence. No product state is changed beyond the disposable local stack.

## Data Requirements

Synthetic principals and review fixtures from the dev blueprint and seed scripts; no real tenant data.

## Role-Based Visibility

Uses synthetic TenantMember and Reviewer principals in two tenants (existing dev blueprint principals plus any Phase B additions).

## Non-Functional Expectations

Deterministic and repeatable from a clean environment; total runtime recorded. Browser matrix for this proof: at least Chromium and Firefox (A1 covers the full support list).

## Dependencies

S0001–S0008; dev authentik blueprint; existing review fixtures (F0001-S0004, F0002).

## Out of Scope

§121.2 items 1–7 and 10–12 beyond what F0001/F0002 already proved (F0018, F0022, F0026); production ingress.

## Questions & Assumptions

No open business-rule question. The end-to-end tool (Playwright is proposed in BLUEPRINT §2.1), how the upstream failure is injected, and how IdP-side refresh grants are counted are Phase B decisions.

## Definition of Done

- [ ] Every AC above has a concrete observed proof in the later feature run.
- [ ] Evidence package contains storage inspections, IdP-side observations and audit rows for each AC.
- [ ] ADR-0051 acceptance is presented to the operator with this evidence (acceptance itself is a separate operator decision).
- [ ] Documentation and tracker state reflect observed results.
- [ ] Required story-level review provenance is appended to STATUS.md in the feature action.
